Oy! Never mind  that nothing has been said about {zo'e} for many moons, we now 
seem to have wandered from the question of what {lo cinfo} refers to to question 
of disparate identity.  So, to try to state my position clearly -- leaving 
aside, for the moment, the identity question -- I say that anything that cinfo 
can be a referent of an occurrence of {lo cinfo}, which, with you guys 
apparently immediately raises the issue of what cinfo.  To answer: 1) lions 2) 
temporally defined lion segments 3) some spatially defined lion segments (those 
that still constitute a functioning organism, those that constitute a 
presentation of a lion when we are being hypercorrect about what we see, but not 
yet slopping into phenomenology) *4) maybe visual sensations of lions with the 
possibility of non-veridical perception (why we use {tu'a} and whatever that 
gadri is in these cases).  Things that don't cinfo: a) lion (as in the deli 
order "A half pound of lion, please") b) Lion(kind) per se (though there might 
be something like conjunctive predication for its manifestations, the same might 
hold for lion and its normative slices) c) lionness.  Each of these is just the 
wrong sort of thing: abstract or disorganized.
That being said, it seems that most of what you have said fits in fairly 
nicely.  As for the issue of identity, I suppose we use common sense, which 
amounts to observed or tightly inferred continuity.  So, if we see a lion on the 
left and one on the right, they are not identical and are, therefore, two.  If 
we see one side of a lion and walk around it, keeping it in sight, we take all 
of the presentations to be of the same lion.  And so on, to the hard cases.

